  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "GgEW0nvhvgV+V9pmV3Kh+IPR2ek=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:48.033Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Because metrics collection is performance-sensitive, the analytics engine ships a benchmark suite comparing its store implementations, **run on every publish** with major-release results kept in the repository.\n\nThe headline: `MemoryAnalyticsStore` and `PostgresAnalyticsStore` are \"very close in performance, with the Postgres implementation slightly faster in most applications.\"\n\n**Query-list benchmark** \u2014 the 600+ most frequent real-world queries against matching tables of around **200k records**: on average the memory (PGlite) store took about **1.36\u00d7** the time of the Postgres store.\n\n**WASM benchmark** \u2014 startup and insert cost for the memory store:\n\n| Operation | Ops/sec | Average time |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Init | 3 | 323.00 ms |\n| 100 inserts | 478 | 2.08 ms |\n| 200k inserts | 0 | 4448.10 ms |\n\nRead together, those numbers say something useful about design choices: a **one-off 323 ms** initialisation and a 36% query penalty is a reasonable price for running the same analytics code in a browser with no server \u2014 which is exactly the trade the vault's own PGlite-based semantic search makes. The 200k-insert figure is the one to respect: bulk backfill into a WASM database is measured in seconds, so a processor replaying long history should expect it.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_06-Analytics Engine/typescript/benchmarks.md` \u2014 Benchmarks: summary, Query-list and WASM tables\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:54:48.032Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:48.033Z"
    }
  },
  {
    "index": 4,
